["<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>You will see most deer for two seconds, half hidden, from the side or from behind. Learn every angle here so your eyes know the animal before your brain does.</p></aside><ul><li>This session is pictures first. Each screen shows one view: side, front, rear, running, bedded, in cover, far away.</li><li>Read the caption, then look for the one or two marks it names.</li><li>BC has three deer you can hunt: mule deer, white tailed deer and black tailed deer <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>The law treats black tailed deer as mule deer: the synopsis says \"Mule (Black tailed) Deer\" (99%, page 4, 2 October 2026 edition).</li><li>Photos are illustrations. The written field marks are the source of truth.</li><li>Photos come from many places in western North America. Captions say where.</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-md-buck-side.jpg\" alt=\"Side view, California. White rump, thin tail with a black tip, grey coat.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Side view, California. White rump, thin tail with a black tip, grey coat. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Marshal Hedin from San Diego, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mule_deer_buck_(Odocoileus_hemionus),_Little_Shasta,_Siskiyou_County_CA_(24178985295).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-md-buck-front.jpg\" alt=\"Front view, North Dakota. Ears out wide like a mule. Antlers fork, then fork again.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Front view, North Dakota. Ears out wide like a mule. Antlers fork, then fork again. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Matt Reinbold from USA, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:I_Think_He%27s_Noticed_Me_(3875851465).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/mule-deer-buck.jpg\" alt=\"Big ears, forked antlers, rope tail with a black tip.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Big ears, forked antlers, rope tail with a black tip. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: GlacierNPS, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mule_Deer_Buck_(43010371115).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-md-buck-4pt.jpg\" alt=\"Side on in snow, Oregon. Practise counting the tips on the near antler.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Side on in snow, Oregon. Practise counting the tips on the near antler. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Oregon Department of Fish &amp; Wildlife, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mule_buck_elk_creek_m_myatt_(5489214303).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Ears: huge, about three quarters the length of the head <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Antlers: each main beam splits into two, and each branch can split again <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Face: pale, with a dark patch on the forehead in fall (Tip).</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-md-buck-rear.jpg\" alt=\"Buck from behind, Colorado. Big white rump patch, thin rope tail with a black tip.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Buck from behind, Colorado. Big white rump patch, thin rope tail with a black tip. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWS Mountain-Prairie, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mule_Deer_Buck_(15382465555).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-md-doe-fawns-rear.jpg\" alt=\"Doe and two fawns, Wyoming. Every rump is white, every tail tip is black.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Doe and two fawns, Wyoming. Every rump is white, every tail tip is black. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWS Mountain-Prairie, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mule_Deer_Doe_and_Fawns_SW_Wyoming_(23204526004).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-md-doe-sage-snow.jpg\" alt=\"Doe in snowy sagebrush, Wyoming. Grey winter coat, tail held down.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Doe in snowy sagebrush, Wyoming. Grey winter coat, tail held down. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWS Mountain-Prairie, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mule_Deer_Doe_in_Southwest_Wyoming_(23267627529).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>The rear view is the fastest ID: white rump, small white tail, black tip <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>A mule deer tail hangs down, even when the deer runs <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-md-bound.jpg\" alt=\"Doe bounding across a road, Utah. Legs tucked, a stiff legged bounce.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Doe bounding across a road, Utah. Legs tucked, a stiff legged bounce. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: BLMUtah, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mule_deer_crossing_the_road_(53658855485).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-md-run-away.jpg\" alt=\"Running away, California. Tail stays down. A dark tail like this may mean a black tailed deer or a cross; a true mule deer tail is white with a black tip.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Running away, California. Tail stays down. A dark tail like this may mean a black tailed deer or a cross; a true mule deer tail is white with a black tip. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Wingchi Poon, CC BY-SA 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deer_running_away.JPG\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>A spooked mule deer often <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Stot\">stots</button>: all four feet hit and push off together, like a pogo stick <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>It often stops on a ridge and looks back (Tip).</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-md-buck-bedded.jpg\" alt=\"Buck bedded in sagebrush, Wyoming. Antlers show; the body hides.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Buck bedded in sagebrush, Wyoming. Antlers show; the body hides. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWS Mountain-Prairie, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mule_Deer_Buck_in_Southwest_Wyoming_(23007137314).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-md-does-bedded.jpg\" alt=\"Two deer bedded in snowy sage. Look for ears above the brush.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Two deer bedded in snowy sage. Look for ears above the brush. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWS Mountain-Prairie, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mule_Deer_Bedded_Down_in_Sagebrush_and_Snow_(15523121163).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-md-fawn-bedded.jpg\" alt=\"Fawn bedded in grass. Only the head and ears show. Leave fawns alone.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Fawn bedded in grass. Only the head and ears show. Leave fawns alone. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWS Mountain Prairie, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mule_deer_fawn_(50131622062).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-md-doe-fawn-cover.jpg\" alt=\"Doe and fawn in brush, Wyoming. In cover you see ears and a face first.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Doe and fawn in brush, Wyoming. In cover you see ears and a face first. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWS Mountain-Prairie, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mule_deer_doe_and_fawns_on_Seedskadee_National_Wildlife_Refuge_01_(15334284900).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-md-buck-cover.jpg\" alt=\"Buck in brush, Oregon. Pick out the antler tines against the twigs.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Buck in brush, Oregon. Pick out the antler tines against the twigs. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service Headquarters, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mule_deer_buck_at_Malheur_Refuge_(5448494556).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-md-buck-sage.jpg\" alt=\"Buck in sagebrush, Idaho. The grey coat matches the sage. Find the white rump.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Buck in sagebrush, Idaho. The grey coat matches the sage. Find the white rump. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: NPS Photo, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_most_commonly_seen_large_mammal_at_Craters_of_the_Moon,_mule_deer_congregate_where_grasses_and_shrubs_dominate._(78fc15f6-e7ed-4ab1-9701-a68cd124c383).JPG\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>In cover, look for parts, not a whole deer: an ear, the white throat, a leg, an antler tip (Tip).</li><li>Sagebrush, bunchgrass and open pine are classic mule deer country in the BC Interior <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-md-distance.jpg\" alt=\"Several mule deer in sagebrush, eastern Oregon. Grey shapes with white rumps.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Several mule deer in sagebrush, eastern Oregon. Grey shapes with white rumps. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: BLM Oregon &amp; Washington, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mule_deer_in_eastern_Oregon_(29844645086).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-md-buck-open.jpg\" alt=\"Buck crossing open flats, Yellowstone. Antlers above the ears, white rump.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Buck crossing open flats, Yellowstone. Antlers above the ears, white rump. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Yellowstone National Park, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mule_deer_buck_and_Roosevelt_Arch_(37686230234).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-md-radium.jpg\" alt=\"Three mule deer near Radium, BC. Open pine and grass winter range.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Three mule deer near Radium, BC. Open pine and grass winter range. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Sir-Mister-Horse, CC BY 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RIMG0316.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Past 300 m (328 yd), use binoculars, then a spotting scope. Never use the rifle scope to look at deer (Tip).</li><li>Colour fools you at distance. Shape, rump and ear size are steadier clues (Tip).</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-md-doe-front.jpg\" alt=\"Doe, front view, Wyoming. No antlers, huge ears, dark forehead.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Doe, front view, Wyoming. No antlers, huge ears, dark forehead. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWS Mountain-Prairie, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mule_Deer_Doe_on_Seedskadee_National_Wildlife_Refuge_(22181735559).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-md-fawn.jpg\" alt=\"Fawn in summer, North Dakota. White spots on a red brown coat.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Fawn in summer, North Dakota. White spots on a red brown coat. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: James St. John, CC BY 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Odocoileus_hemionus_(mule_deer)_3_(8270240814).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-md-young-bucks.jpg\" alt=\"Three young bucks, South Dakota. Small antlers. Count every deer in a group.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Three young bucks, South Dakota. Small antlers. Count every deer in a group. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: NPS photo, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mule_deer_are_commonly_seen_around_the_park,_especially_near_the_campground_and_visitor_center._Also_called_blacktail_deer,_they_(95abdd64-155d-4519-3ebc-81b1b3cab293).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>A doe has no antlers. A <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Button buck\">Button buck</button> (male fawn) has only bumps under the hair <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Fawns lose their spots by early fall <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-wt-buck-side.jpg\" alt=\"Side view, New Jersey. Brown tail with white edges; tines rise off one main beam.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Side view, New Jersey. Brown tail with white edges; tines rise off one main beam. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Famartin, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2025-11-09_07_48_17_White-tailed_Deer_buck_in_autumn_along_Aquetong_Lane_in_the_Mountainview_section_of_Ewing_Township,_Mercer_County,_New_Jersey.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-wt-buck-front.jpg\" alt=\"Front view. White nose band and throat patch; beams sweep forward.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Front view. White nose band and throat patch; beams sweep forward. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Famartin, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2024-11-08_10_54_30_White-tailed_Deer_buck_along_Montague_Avenue_in_the_Mountainview_section_of_Ewing_Township,_Mercer_County,_New_Jersey.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-wt-buck-flag.jpg\" alt=\"Running in snow, West Virginia. Tail up, white underneath: the flag.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Running in snow, West Virginia. Tail up, white underneath: the flag. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: ForestWander, CC BY-SA 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Whitetail-buck-running-snow_-_West_Virginia_-_ForestWander.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>White tail antlers: one main beam with single tines growing up from it. They do not fork like a mule deer's <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Ears are smaller than a mule deer's <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-wt-doe.jpg\" alt=\"Doe, side view, in snow. Long, wide brown tail.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Doe, side view, in snow. Long, wide brown tail. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service - Midwest Region, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:White-tailed_deer_(54365597311).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-wt-doe-flag.jpg\" alt=\"Doe trotting with the white [[Flag|flag]] up.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Doe trotting with the white <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Flag\">flag</button> up. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: HARRY W. McCORMACK ( TEABERRYEAGLE ), CC BY 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EASTERN_WHITETAIL_DEER_-_A_DOE_WITH_TAIL_UP,_THE_WHITE_FLAG_OF_ALARM.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-wt-run.jpg\" alt=\"White tails bounding away, Montana. Long, low gallop, tails up.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>White tails bounding away, Montana. Long, low gallop, tails up. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: NPS, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2_White-tailed_deer_run_past_the_Indian_Memorial._(74cf0194-1dd8-b71b-0b1f-3242b5474cf7).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>White tails gallop with the tail raised <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>. Mule deer bounce with the tail down <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-md-buck-rear.jpg\" alt=\"Mule deer rear: white rump, thin tail, black tip.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Mule deer rear: white rump, thin tail, black tip. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWS Mountain-Prairie, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mule_Deer_Buck_(15382465555).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-wt-buck-flag.jpg\" alt=\"White tail rear: wide tail, white underside, raised when alarmed.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>White tail rear: wide tail, white underside, raised when alarmed. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: ForestWander, CC BY-SA 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Whitetail-buck-running-snow_-_West_Virginia_-_ForestWander.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-md-buck-front.jpg\" alt=\"Mule deer antlers: forks on forks.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Mule deer antlers: forks on forks. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Matt Reinbold from USA, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:I_Think_He%27s_Noticed_Me_(3875851465).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-wt-buck-front.jpg\" alt=\"White tail antlers: tines off one beam.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>White tail antlers: tines off one beam. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Famartin, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2024-11-08_10_54_30_White-tailed_Deer_buck_along_Montague_Avenue_in_the_Mountainview_section_of_Ewing_Township,_Mercer_County,_New_Jersey.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div>","<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Clue</th><th>Mule deer</th><th>White tailed deer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tail</td><td>Thin, white, black tip <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>Wide, brown on top, white below <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Ears</td><td>Very large <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>Medium <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Antlers</td><td>Fork, then fork again <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>Tines off one main beam <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Running</td><td>Stots, tail down <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>Gallops, flag up <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-bt-buck.jpg\" alt=\"Buck in a river, Washington. Smaller, redder deer; forked antlers.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Buck in a river, Washington. Smaller, redder deer; forked antlers. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Philip Cohen, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Columbian_Black-tailed_Deer_buck.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-bt-doe.jpg\" alt=\"Doe, Washington. The whole top of the tail is dark.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Doe, Washington. The whole top of the tail is dark. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Walter Siegmund (talk), CC BY-SA 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Odocoileus_hemionus_5432.JPG\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-bt-victoria.jpg\" alt=\"Young buck, Victoria, BC. Wide tail held down, dark on top.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Young buck, Victoria, BC. Wide tail held down, dark on top. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Gordon Leggett, CC BY 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2025-04-13_01_Black-tailed_deer_(Odocoileus_hemionus_columbianus)_%E2%80%93_Victoria_BC_Canada.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-bt-bedded.jpg\" alt=\"Young buck bedded by a highway, Ucluelet, Vancouver Island.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Young buck bedded by a highway, Ucluelet, Vancouver Island. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Buiobuione, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black-tailed_deer_-_Odocoileus_hemionus_columbianus_-_05.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-bt-buck-velvet.jpg\" alt=\"Buck in velvet, Mount Rainier. Antlers fork like a mule deer's.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Buck in velvet, Mount Rainier. Antlers fork like a mule deer's. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Charles J. Sharp, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Columbian_black-tailed_deer_(Odocoileus_hemionus_columbianus)_male_Mt_Rainier.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Black tailed deer live on the coast and in the Fraser Valley, the deer you would meet around Mission <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>","<ul><li>Tail: wider than a mule deer's and dark or black down the whole top (Tip).</li></ul><ul><li>Antlers fork like a mule deer's, so the 4 point rules apply to them too (99%, page 4).</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-md-buck-front.jpg\" alt=\"Many tines a side. Count one antler and leave out the brow tine.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Many tines a side. Count one antler and leave out the brow tine. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Matt Reinbold from USA, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:I_Think_He%27s_Noticed_Me_(3875851465).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-md-buck-small.jpg\" alt=\"Young buck, Yellowstone. Fewer than 4 tines a side. Not a 4 point buck.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Young buck, Yellowstone. Fewer than 4 tines a side. Not a 4 point buck. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Yellowstone National Park from Yellowstone NP, USA, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mule_deer_buck_(15877888045).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-md-forkhorn.jpg\" alt=\"Fork horn in velvet, Yosemite. 2 points a side. Not a 4 point buck.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Fork horn in velvet, Yosemite. 2 points a side. Not a 4 point buck. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Guywelch2000, CC0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mule_deer_buck_in_velvet_Mariposa_Grove.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-md-young-bucks.jpg\" alt=\"Small antlers on all three. Not 4 point bucks.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Small antlers on all three. Not 4 point bucks. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: NPS photo, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mule_deer_are_commonly_seen_around_the_park,_especially_near_the_campground_and_visitor_center._Also_called_blacktail_deer,_they_(95abdd64-155d-4519-3ebc-81b1b3cab293).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><aside class=\"callout warn\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Study aid only. The official regulations are the law.</div></aside>","<aside class=\"callout law\"><div class=\"callout-t\">\"MULE (BLACK-TAILED) DEER - FOUR POINTS OR GREATER BUCK - means any buck having at least four tines, excluding the brow tine, on one antler.\"</div></aside><ul><li>Plain English: count one antler. Four or more true tines, not counting the <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Brow tine\">brow tine</button>, and he is a <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"4 point buck\">4 point buck</button> (99%, page 4, 2 October 2026 edition).</li></ul><ul><li>A <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Tine\">Tine</button> must be at least 2.5 cm long and longer than it is wide (99%, page 4).</li></ul><ul><li>A spike or a fork horn (2 points) or a 3 point is not a 4 point buck (99%, page 4).</li></ul>","<ul><li>Some seasons are for 4 point bucks only. The class column of the season table for your MU (Management Unit) says which (90%, my reading).</li></ul><ul><li>Cannot count the points for sure? Do not shoot (Tip).</li></ul>","<aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>Look at the back end first, then the head. The tail tells you the species, the antlers tell you if he is legal.</p></aside>","<aside class=\"callout mistake\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Calling a deer from its colour. Grey, brown and red coats all happen in every species.</li><li>Counting the brow tine on a mule deer. It is excluded from the 4 point count.</li><li>Shooting at a deer in cover when you only saw a leg or an ear. Wait for the whole animal.</li></ul></aside>","<aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><ul><li>Every deer you see this week, say out loud: species, sex, and which view you had.</li><li>For every buck, count the points on the near antler through binoculars before you touch the rifle.</li><li>Note which mark you saw first. Most days it will be the rump or the ears.</li></ul></aside>","<ul><li>BC <a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/sports-recreation-arts-and-culture/outdoor-recreation/fishing-and-hunting/hunting/regulations/hunting-trapping-synopsis.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hunting and Trapping Regulations Synopsis 2026 to 2028, 2 October 2026 edition</a>, read directly: mule (black tailed) deer 4 point definition and tine definition, page 4. Checked 2026-10-02 <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>.</li><li>Alberta Institute for Wildlife Conservation, <a href=\"https://www.aiwc.ca/blog/spotting-the-difference-a-guide-to-albertas-mule-deer-and-whitetails/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mule deer versus white tail</a>: tail, ears, antlers, running. Checked 2026-10-01 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>BC Ministry of Environment, <a href=\"https://www.env.gov.bc.ca/wld/documents/muledeer.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Mule and black tailed deer in BC</a>, search preview: range of black tailed deer. Checked 2026-10-03 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Field marks also follow the Mule deer and White tailed deer sessions in this app.</li><li>Photos: Wikimedia Commons, public domain or Creative Commons. Credits under each photo.</li></ul>"]